\\\\{1,\\\\dots,n\\\\}$ such that the unique uncovered square in row $i$ is at column $p(i)$.  We denote this square by $U_i=(i,p(i))$.\\n\\nFor each row $i$ define the *left interval*\\n\\\\[L_i=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le j<p(i)\\\\},\\\\]\\nand the *right interval*\\n\\\\[R_i=\\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid p(i)<j\\\\le n\\\\}.\\\\]\\nThus $L_i$ (resp. $R_i$) consists of the unit squares in row $i$ that lie strictly to the left (resp. to the right) of the uncovered square $U_i$.\\n\\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Lemma 1 (critical squares).**\\nFor every row $i$ with $p(i)>1$ the square $C_i^{-}=(i,p(i)-1)$ is called the *left‑critical* square of row $i$; for every row $i$ with $p(i)<n$ the square $C_i^{+}=(i,p(i)+1)$ is the *right‑critical* square of row $i$.  There are exactly $2n-2$ critical squares (the two rows whose uncovered square lies on a border contribute only one critical square each).\\n\\n*Proof.*  The statement about the number of critical squares follows directly from the fact that $p$ is a bijection: exactly one row has $p(i)=1$ and exactly one row has $p(i)=n$.\\n\\nNow consider any rectangle $\\\\mathcal R$ whose interior contains no uncovered square $U_i$.  Suppose $\\\\mathcal R$ contained two distinct critical squares, say $C_i^{-}$ and $C_k^{-}$ with $i\\\\neq k$.  Then the column interval of $\\\\mathcal R$ must contain both columns $p(i)-1$ and $p(k)-1$.  Without loss of generality assume $p(i)<p(k)$.  Because the column interval is contiguous, it also contains column $p(i)$, which is the column of $U_i$.  Since row $i$ belongs to $\\\\mathcal R$, the square $U_i$ would lie inside $\\\\mathcal R$, contradicting the definition of a tile.  The same argument works for a pair of right‑critical squares, and a left‑critical together with a right‑critical square would force the inclusion of the uncovered square of the row that supplies the left‑critical square.  Hence **any** admissible rectangle can contain **at most one