ince the column interval is contiguous, it also contains column \\(p(i)\\).  Row \\(i\\) belongs to \\(R\\) (because \\(C_i^{-}\\) lies in row \\(i\\)), so the square \\((i,p(i))=U_i\\) lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) avoids uncovered squares.\n\n- **Both right\u2011critical.**  The argument is symmetric: if \\(R\\) contains \\(C_i^{+}=(i,p(i)+1)\\) and \\(C_k^{+}=(k,p(k)+1)\\) with \\(p(i)<p(k)\\), then the column interval contains \\(p(i)\\) and row \\(i\\) is present, so \\(U_i\\) is inside \\(R\\).\n\n- **One left\u2011critical and one right\u2011critical.**  Suppose \\(R\\) contains \\(C_i^{-}\\) and \\(C_k^{+}\\).  The column interval of \\(R\\) contains both \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(k)+1\\).  If \\(p(i)\\le p(k)\\) then the interval also contains column \\(p(i)\\); together with row \\(i\\) this yields \\(U_i\\in R\\).  If \\(p(i)>p(k)\\) then the interval contains column \\(p(k)\\) and row \\(k\\), giving \\(U_k\\in R\\).  In either subcase an uncovered square lies in \\(R\\), a contradiction.\n\nThus no rectangle avoiding uncovered squares can contain two critical squares.  \u220e\n\nFrom Lemma\u00a01 we obtain the following lower bound.\n\n**Corollary 1 (lower bound).** *Every admissible tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.*\nIndeed, each of the \\(2n-2\\) critical squares must be covered by some tile, and Lemma\u00a01 guarantees that a single tile can cover at most one of them.\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nWe now exhibit a tiling that uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\nFix any permutation \\(p\\) (the uncovered squares are \\(U_i=(i,p(i))\\)).  For each row \\(i\\) do the following:\n\n- If \\(p(i)>1\\), place a horizontal tile of size \\(1\\times(p(i)-1)\\) covering precisely the squares of \\(L_i\\).  This tile contains the left\u2011critical square \\(C_i^{-}\\) as its rightmost cell.\n- If \\(p(i)<n\\), place a horizontal tile of size