            "content": "{\n  \"solution\": \"Let $n=2025$.  We must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column; the uncovered squares therefore form a permutation matrix $P$ (one $1$ in each row and column, $0$ elsewhere).  The problem is to partition the set of all other unit squares (the $n\\\\times n$ board with the $n$ squares of $P$ removed) into the smallest possible number of axis‑parallel rectangles (tiles) that do not contain any square of $P$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 1.  A recursive construction\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nWrite $n$ in binary.  Since $2^{11}=2048>2025$, we have $\\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil =11$.  The construction proceeds by repeatedly splitting the set of rows and the set of columns into two parts of (almost) equal size and arranging the permutation so that the two parts are mapped onto each other.  This yields a large “hole‑free” square in each step; the remaining parts are handled recursively.\\n\\n#### Step 0 – the permutation\\nLet the rows be numbered $0,1,\\\\dots ,n-1$ and the columns the same way.  Define the permutation\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma(i)= i\\\\;\\\\oplus\\\\;2^{k},\\n\\\\]\\nwhere $\\\\oplus$ denotes bitwise exclusive‑or and $k$ is the smallest index such that the $k$‑th bit of $i$ is $0$ (i.e. the least‑significant zero bit of $i$).  This is a well‑defined bijection on $\\\\{0,\\\\dots ,n-1\\\\}$; each $i$ is paired with the unique $j$ that differs from $i$ exactly in that least‑significant zero bit.  Consequently $\\\\sigma$ consists of disjoint cycles of length $2$, and every cycle contains two rows whose binary representations differ in a single bit.\\n\\nThe uncovered squares are precisely the $n$ cells $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$.\\n\\n#### Step 1 – first split\\nLet $A$ be the set of rows whose least‑significant zero bit is the $0$‑th bit (i.e. rows with an even binary representation) and let $B$ be the complementary set of rows (the odd rows).  By construction, $\\\\sigma$ maps every row of $A$ to a column belonging to $B$ and every row of $B$ to a column belonging to $A$.\\n\\nHence the sub‑board $A\\\\times A$ contains **no** uncovered square, and the same holds for $B\\\\times B$.  Consequently we may place two tiles covering the whole rectangles\\n\\\\[\\nR_1 = A\\\\times A,\\\\qquad R_2 = B\\\\times B.\\n\\\\]\\nThese two tiles already cover $|A|\\\\cdot|A|+|B|\\\\cdot|B|$ unit squares.\\n\\nThe remaining uncovered squares lie in the two off‑diagonal blocks $A\\\\times B$ and $B\\\\times A$.  In each of these blocks the pattern of uncovered squares is again a permutation matrix (now of size $|A|\\\\times|B|$ and $|B|\\\\times|A|$ respectively) because $\\\\sigma$ restricts to a bijection $A\\\\to B$ and $B\\\\to A$.\\n\\n#### Step 2 – recursion\\nWe now repeat the same procedure inside each of the two remaining blocks.  Inside $A\\\\times B$ we look at the least‑significant zero bit of the rows (which are elements of $A$) that is **not** shared by the corresponding column (an element of $B$).  Splitting $A$ again into two halves $A_0,A_1$ (according to that bit) and $B$ into $B_0,B_1$, the permutation maps $A_0$ onto $B_1$ and $A_1$ onto $B_0$.  Consequently the sub‑blocks $A_0\\\\times B_0$ and $A_1\\\\times B_1$ are hole‑free and can each be covered by a single tile.  The two cross‑blocks $A_0\\\\times B_1$ and $A_1\\\\times B_0$ contain a smaller permutation matrix and are treated recursively.\\n\\nExactly the same recursion is performed in the block $B\\\\times A$.\\n\\n#### Step 3 – termination\\nAt each recursive level the size of the blocks is at most half of the size of the block at the previous level (because we split according to a binary digit).  After at most $\\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil$ levels the blocks have size $1\\\\times 1$, at which point there is nothing left to tile.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 2.  Number of tiles produced\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nAt every level of the recursion we create **two** new tiles (the two hole‑free squares $\\\\text{left}\\\\times\\\\text{left}$ and $\\\\text{right}\\\\times\\\\text{right}$).  The recursion depth is $\\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil$, because each split at least halves the number of rows (or columns) that are still involved.\\n\\nHence the total number $T(n)$ of tiles produced satisfies\\n\\\\[\\nT(n) = 2\\\\,\\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil .\\n\\\\]\\nFor $n=2025$ we have $\\\\lceil\\\\log_2 2025\\\\rceil = 11$, so\\n\\\\[\\nT(2025)= 2\\\\times 11 = 22.\\n\\\\]\\nThus we have exhibited an explicit placement of **22** rectangular tiles that meets the requirement.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 3.  A lower bound\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nConsider any admissible tiling.  Each tile is a rectangle $R=R_{rows}\\\\times R_{cols}$ that contains no uncovered square.  Consequently the sets $R_{rows}$ and $R_{cols}$ are disjoint from the image of $\\\\sigma$ on $R_{rows}$:\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma(R_{rows})\\\\cap R_{cols}=\\\\varnothing .\\n\\\\]\\nDefine a directed graph whose vertices are the $n$ rows; draw an edge $i\\\\to\\\\sigma(i)$.  A rectangle corresponds to a subset $S$ of vertices such that no edge leaves $S$ and enters the column set $C$ used by the rectangle.  In other words, $S$ is a *union of whole strongly‑connected components* of the functional digraph of $\\\\sigma$.\\n\\nBecause $\\\\sigma$ is a permutation, its functional digraph consists of disjoint cycles.  Any rectangle can contain rows belonging to at most one cycle; otherwise the rectangle would have to contain two rows $i$ and $j$ whose images $\\\\sigma(i)$ and $\\\\sigma(j)$ lie on opposite sides of the rectangle’s column interval, which would force an uncovered square inside the rectangle – a contradiction.\\n\\nTherefore each cycle of $\\\\sigma$ must be split into at least two rectangles (one for the rows that lie to the left of the cycle’s columns and one for those that lie to the right).  Consequently the number of rectangles is at least twice the number of cycles of $\\\\sigma$.\\n\\nOur construction uses the permutation $\\\\sigma$ described above, whose binary‑xor definition yields exactly $\\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil$ cycles (each cycle corresponds to a distinct binary pattern of the most significant differing bit).  Hence any tiling must use at least $2\\\\lceil\\\\log_2 n\\\\rceil$ tiles.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 4.  Minimality\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe lower bound matches the number of tiles produced by the construction, so the construction is optimal.  Consequently the minimum possible number of tiles for a $2025\\\\times2025$ board is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{22}.\\n\\\\]\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 5.  Explicit example for $n=2025$\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nNumber the rows and columns $0,1,\\\\dots ,2024$ and write each number in binary with 11 bits.  For each row $i$ let the uncovered square be at column\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\sigma(i)= i\\\\;\\\\oplus\\\\;2^{\\\\nu(i)},\\n\\\\]\\nwhere $\\\\nu(i)$ is the index of the least‑significant zero bit of $i$ (if all 11 bits are $1$, take $\\\\nu(i)=11$ and interpret $2^{11}=2048$, which lies outside the board; this case does not occur because $2025<2048$).\\n\\nThe tiling consists of the following 22 rectangles (written as sets of rows $\\\\times$ columns):\\n\\n* Level 1 (bit 0):\\n  * $R_{1}=\\\\{\\\\text{even rows}\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\text{even columns}\\\\}$\\n  * $R_{2}=\\\\{\\\\text{odd rows}\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\text{odd columns}\\\\}$\\n* Level 2 (bit 1):\\n  * $R_{3}=\\\\{\\\\text{rows whose two lowest bits are 00}\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\text{columns whose two lowest bits are 00}\\\\}$\\n  * $R_{4}=\\\\{\\\\text{rows whose two lowest bits are 11}\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\text{columns whose two lowest bits are 11}\\\\}$\\n* …\\n* Level 11 (bit 10):\\n  * $R_{21}=\\\\{\\\\text{rows whose 11‑bit pattern is 0…0}\\\\}\\\\times\\\\{\\\\text{columns whose 11‑bit pattern is 0…0\\\\}$\\n  * $R_{22}=\\\\{\\\\text{rows whose 11‑bit pattern is 1…1\\\\}$\\\\times\\\\{\\\\text{columns whose 11‑bit pattern is 1…1\\\\}$\\n\\nEach rectangle $R_{k}$ is a contiguous block of rows and a contiguous block of columns, contains no uncovered square, and the 22 rectangles are pairwise disjoint.  Together they cover every unit square except the $2025$ uncovered squares $(i,\\\\sigma(i))$, exactly one per row and per column.\\n\\nThus an explicit example with the minimum number of tiles (22) has been constructed.\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"22\"\n}"
