per bound: a construction with \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles\\n\\nChoose the identity permutation \\\\(p(i)=i\\\\) (the holes lie on the main diagonal).  For each row \\\\(i\\\\) with \\\\(2\\\\le i\\\\le n\\\\) place a horizontal tile\\n\\\\[\\nH_i = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid i<j\\\\le n\\\\},\\n\\\\]\\nwhich covers all squares to the right of the hole in that row.  For each column \\\\(j\\\\) with \\\\(2\\\\le j\\\\le n\\\\) place a vertical tile\\n\\\\[\\nV_j = \\\\{(i,j)\\\\mid 1\\\\le i<j\\\\},\\n\\\\]\\nwhich covers all squares above the hole in that column.\\n\\nThe sets \\\\(H_i\\\\) and \\\\(V_j\\\\) are pairwise disjoint because a square \\\\((i,j)\\\\) with \\\\(i<j\\\\) belongs to exactly one of them: it lies in the horizontal tile of row \\\\(i\\\\) and not in any vertical tile, while a square with \\\\(i>j\\\\) lies in the vertical tile of column \\\\(j\\\\) and not in any horizontal tile.  Every non‑diagonal square is covered, and the diagonal squares remain uncovered.  The number of tiles used is\\n\\\\[\\n|\\\\{H_i\\\\mid 2\\\\le i\\\\le n\\\\}|+|\\\\{V_j\\\\mid 2\\\\le j\\\\le n\\\\}| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\\n\\\\]\\nThus \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles are sufficient.\\n\\n----------------------------------------------------------------\\n#### 2.  A lower bound: at least \\\\(2n-2\\\\) tiles are necessary\\n\\nFor each row \\\\(i\\\\) with \\\\(p(i)\\\\neq 1\\\\) consider the **left‑neighbor cell**\\n\\\\[\\nL_i = (i,\\\\,p(i)-1).\\n\\\\]\\nSimilarly, for each row \\\\(i\\\\) with \\\\(p(i)\\\\neq n\\\\) consider the **right‑neighbor cell**\\n\\\\[\\nR_i = (i,\\\\,p(i)+1).\\n\\\\]\\nBecause the permutation is a bijection, at most one row can satisfy \\\\(p(i)=1\\\\) and at most one row can satisfy \\\\(p(i)=n\\\\).  Hence there are at least \\\\(n-1\\\\) distinct left‑neighbor cells and at least \\\\(n-1\\\\) distinct right‑neighbor cells, altogether at least \\\\(2n-2\\\\) distinguished cells.\\n\\nWe claim that **no tile can contain two distinct distinguished cells**.\\n\\n*T