es lie on the main diagonal).  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) place a horizontal tile\n\\[\nH_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers all squares to the right of the hole in that row.  For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile\n\\[\nV_j = \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\},\n\\]\nwhich covers all squares above the hole in that column.\n\nThe sets \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) are pairwise disjoint because a square \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\) belongs to exactly one of them: it lies in the horizontal tile of row \\(i\\) and not in any vertical tile, while a square with \\(i>j\\) lies in the vertical tile of column \\(j\\) and not in any horizontal tile.  Every non\u2011diagonal square is covered, and the diagonal squares remain uncovered.  The number of tiles used is\n\\[\n|\\{H_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}|+|\\{V_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}| = (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\nThus \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are necessary\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)\\neq 1\\) consider the **left\u2011neighbor cell**\n\\[\nL_i = (i,\\,p(i)-1).\n\\]\nSimilarly, for each row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)\\neq n\\) consider the **right\u2011neighbor cell**\n\\[\nR_i = (i,\\,p(i)+1).\n\\]\nBecause the permutation is a bijection, at most one row can satisfy \\(p(i)=1\\) and at most one row can satisfy \\(p(i)=n\\).  Hence there are at least \\(n-1\\) distinct left\u2011neighbor cells and at least \\(n-1\\) distinct right\u2011neighbor cells, altogether at least \\(2n-2\\) distinguished cells.\n\nWe claim that **no tile can contain two distinct distinguished cells**.\n\n*Two left\u2011neighbors.*  Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).  Without loss of generality let \\(i<j\\) and assume \\(p(i)<p(j)\\) (the other case is symmetric).  The column interval of \\(R\\) contains both \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\); therefore it also contains the column \\(p(i)\\).  Since row \\(i\\) belongs to \\(R\\), the square \\((i,p(i))\\) \u2013 which is a hole \u2013 lies inside \\(R\\), contradicting the requirement that tiles avoid uncovered squares.  Hence a rectangle cannot contain two left\u2011neighbor cells.\n\n*Two right\u2011neighbors.*  The argument is analogous: if \\(R\\) contained \\(R_i\\) and \\(R_j\\) with \\(i<j\\) and \\(p(i)>p(j)\\), the column interval would contain \\(p(i)\\) and thus the hole \\((i,p(i))\\).\n\n*One left\u2011 and one right\u2011neighbor.*  Suppose \\(R\\) contains \\(L_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\(R_j=(j,p(j)+1)\\).  The column interval of \\(R\\) contains both \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)+1\\); consequently it contains the column \\(p(i)\\).  Row \\(i\\) is in \\(R\\), so the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) lies inside \\(R\\), again a contradiction.\n\nThus each tile can cover **at most one** of the \\(2n-2\\) distinguished cells.  Consequently any tiling satisfying the problem\u2019s conditions must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe construction of Section\u202f1 uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the argument of Section\u202f2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Answer:** 4048",
