            "content": "{\n  \"solution\": \"Let $n=2025$.\\n\\nA placement of the uncovered unit squares must contain exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column, so the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there is a bijection $p\\\\colon\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}\\\\to\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}$ such that the uncovered square in row $i$ is $(i,p(i))$.\\n\\nA tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose set of rows $R$ and set of columns $C$ satisfy\\n\\\\[\\nR\\\\times C\\\\subseteq \\\\{(i,j):j\\\\neq p(i)\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nHence for every $i\\\\in R$ we must have $p(i)\\\\notin C$, and for every $j\\\\in C$ we must have $p^{-1}(j)\\\\notin R$.\\nBecause $p$ is a permutation, the condition $p(i)\\\\notin C$ for all $i\\\\in R$ is equivalent to\\n\\\\[\\n|R|+|C|\\\\le n. \\\\tag{1}\\n\\\\]\\n(If $|R|+|C|>n$, by the pigeon‑hole principle some $i\\\\in R$ would satisfy $p(i)\\\\in C$, contradicting the definition of a tile.)\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 1.  A lower bound for the number of tiles\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nConsider any tiling that satisfies the requirement.  Split the board into the two triangular regions\\n\\\\[\\nU=\\\\{(i,j):i<j\\\\},\\\\qquad L=\\\\{(i,j):i>j\\\\}.\\n\\\\]\\nEvery tile lies entirely in $U$, entirely in $L$, or is a single column (or a single row) that avoids the diagonal; it can never contain a cell $(i,i)$ because those cells are uncovered.\\n\\nLet a tile $T$ be situated in $U$.  Then all rows of $T$ are smaller than all columns of $T$; consequently $|R_T|\\\\le n-1$ and $|C_T|\\\\le n-1$, and by (1) we have $|R_T|+|C_T|\\\\le n$.\\nBecause the rows of $U$ are $1,2,\\\\dots ,n-1$, each row $i$ must be covered by tiles whose total number of columns equals $n-i$ (the cells of $U$ in that row).  Using (1) one checks that a single tile can contribute at most $n-i$ columns to row $i$ only when $|R_T|=1$; otherwise at least two tiles are needed for that row.  Hence every row $i$ with $1\\\\le i\\\\le n-1$ needs at least one distinct tile in $U$.  The same argument applied to $L$ shows that every column $j$ with $1\\\\le j\\\\le n-1$ needs at least one distinct tile in $L$.\\nTherefore any admissible tiling uses at least $(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nTake the *identity* permutation $p(i)=i$; the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.  For each $k=2,3,\\\\dots ,n$ place two vertical tiles:\\n\\n* a tile covering rows $k,\\\\dots ,n$ and column $k-1$;\\n* a tile covering rows $1,\\\\dots ,k-1$ and column $k$.\\n\\nThe first family of tiles occupies the part of the board strictly below the diagonal, the second family occupies the part strictly above the diagonal.  Because the column of a tile never coincides with the diagonal cell of any of its rows, each tile respects condition (1).  All cells of the board except the diagonal are covered, and the diagonal cells remain uncovered, giving exactly one uncovered square in each row and each column.\\n\\nThe construction uses $2(n-1)=2n-2$ tiles.  For $n=2025$ this is\\n\\\\[\\n2\\\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\\n\\\\]\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n### 3.  Minimality\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nThe lower bound proved in Section 1 shows that no tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, while the construction of Section 2 shows that $2n-2$ tiles are sufficient.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly $2n-2$.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\n**Answer**\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\\nFor a $2025\\\\times2025$ grid the minimum number of tiles required is\\n\\\\[\\n\\\\boxed{4048}.\\n\\\\]\",\n  \"final_answer\": \"4048\"\n}"
