ntly a tile that uses column $k$ can use **only one** column of $L$, because if it used two distinct columns $k<\\ell$ then its rows would have to be $>\\ell$, contradicting the presence of column $k$.\nThus each column $k$ of $L$ must be covered by a distinct tile.  Hence at least $n-1$ tiles are needed to cover the whole lower\u2011triangular part $L$.\n\nExactly the same argument applied to the **upper** triangular part\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}\n\\]\nshows that at least $n-1$ tiles are required there as well.\nTherefore any tiling satisfying the condition uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{2}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nTake the identity permutation, i.e. keep the diagonal cells $(i,i)$ uncovered.\nFor each $k=2,3,\\dots ,n$ place two vertical tiles:\n\n* **Tile $A_k$** covering rows $k,\\dots ,n$ and column $k-1$;\n* **Tile $B_k$** covering rows $1,\\dots ,k-1$ and column $k$.\n\nTile $A_k$ lies completely below the diagonal because every row it uses is $\\ge k$ while its column is $k-1<k$; tile $B_k$ lies completely above the diagonal because every row it uses is $\\le k-1$ while its column is $k>k-1$.  In both cases the rows and columns are disjoint, so condition (1) is satisfied.\nAll cells of the board except the diagonal are covered by exactly one of these $2(n-1)$ tiles, and the diagonal cells remain uncovered.  Hence each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered unit square.\nThe construction therefore uses exactly $2n-2$ tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Minimality\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nInequality (2) shows that no admissible tiling can use fewer than $2n-2$ tiles, while the construction above shows that $2n-2$ tiles are sufficient.  Consequently the minimum possibl