ing column k. Let's examine: Suppose a tile covers cells in column k (some rows > k). Since rows must be > k to avoid diagonal, the tile's rows are a set R subset of {k+1,...,n}. The tile's columns set C must be a set of columns, but they claim a tile can only use one column of L. Because if tile uses two distinct columns k and ℓ (kℓ), then rows must be > ℓ (since rows must be > each column index to avoid diagonal). But then rows cannot include rows between k+1 and ℓ (including row ℓ?), but they could still be > ℓ. However tile's rows must be a contiguous set? Actually a tile is a rectangle: rows set R is a contiguous interval of rows, columns set C is a contiguous interval of columns. So if C includes k and ℓ, then C includes all columns between k and ℓ. Then rows must be > ℓ to avoid diagonal for column ℓ. But then for column k, rows > ℓ also > k, so okay. However the rectangle would also include column k+1,...,ℓ-1, which are also columns of L (since row indices > column indices). But those columns are also in L, so the tile would cover cells in those columns as well, which would be multiple columns of L. So the claim that a tile can only use one column of L might be false. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nThe tile is a rectangle defined by rows R = {r1,...,r2} (contiguous) and columns C = {c1,...,c2} (contiguous). The condition that the rectangle does not intersect the diagonal means that for any (i,j) with i in R and j in C, we must have i != j. Actually they require R x C subset of cells not on diagonal. That means for any i in R and j in C, i != j. So the rectangle cannot contain any cell where row = column. That is, the rectangle must not intersect the diagonal line i=j. So the rectangle must lie entirely either above the diagonal (ij for all cells) or below (i>j for all cells), or be a single row or column that avoids its diagonal cell. Because if rectangle spans both above and below, it would cross diagonal. Indeed, if rectangle includes rows both less than some column and greater than some column, there would be a cell where i=j. So the rectangle must be wholly above or wholly below diagonal, or be a single row or column that avoids diagonal cell. That seems correct.\n\nNow consider a tile that lies below diagonal: then for all (i,j) in R x C, i > j. So the smallest row index in R must be > largest column index in C. So min(R) > max(C). Similarly, for tile above diagonal: max(R) min(C). So the rectangle is \"strictly below\" diagonal if the entire set of rows is greater than all columns.\n\nNow consider a tile below diagonal covering column k (i.e., column k is in C). Since tile is below diagonal, we have min(R) > max(C). Since max(C) >= k (since column k is in C, and C may include columns > k). Actually if C includes column k and maybe others > k, then max(C) >= k. So min(R) > max(C) >= k, so min(R) > k. So all rows in R are > k. So rows are > k. So the tile's rows are a contiguous interval of rows all > k. The tile's columns are a contiguous interval of columns that includes k and possibly others > k. But if C includes any column ℓ > k, then max(C) >= ℓ, and min(R) > ℓ as well. So rows are > ℓ. So the rectangle would be entirely below diagonal for column ℓ as well. So it could include multiple columns of L. So the claim that a tile can only use one column of L is not correct: a tile below diagonal could span multiple columns, as long as rows are all > max(C). For example, consider n=5, tile covering rows 4-5 and columns 2-3. This rectangle includes cells (4,2),(4,3),(5,2),(5,3). All those are below diagonal because rows > columns. So tile uses two columns of L (2 and 3). So the claim that each column of L must be covered by a distinct tile is false. Indeed, a single tile can cover multiple columns of L as long as it is a rectangle below diagonal. So the lower bound argument is flawed.\n\nThus the lower bound of n-1 tiles for lower triangle is not valid. The construction uses exactly 2(n-1) tiles, but perhaps fewer tiles could cover the lower triangle with fewer than n-1 tiles. Let's examine if we can cover lower triangle with fewer than n-1 tiles while respecting constraints. The lower triangle has (n choose 2) cells. Each tile below diagonal can be any rectangle with rows > columns. The maximum number of cells a tile can cover is limited by the shape: if we choose rows from r to n and columns from 1 to c, with r > c, then the tile covers (n-r+1)*(c-1+1) = (n-r+1)*c cells. To maximize cells per tile, we could take r = c+1 (the smallest possible row index > max column) to maximize rows count? Actually we want to maximize area: choose rows as many as possible (i.e., r minimal) and columns as many as possible (c maximal) while maintaining r > c. The largest rectangle below diagonal is when r = c+1, and we take rows r..n (size n-c) and columns 1..c (size c). So area = c*(n-c). This is maximized when c ≈ n/2, giving area about (n/2)*(n/2) = n^2/4. So a single tile can cover up to roughly n^2/4 cells. The lower triangle has n(n-1)/2 ≈ n^2/2 cells. So at least 2 tiles are needed to cover lower triangle. So the lower bound of n-1 is far from optimal. So the solution's lower bound is not correct.\n\nThus the answer 2n-2 may not be minimal; perhaps fewer tiles can achieve the condition. Let's think about the problem more: We need each row and each column to have exactly one uncovered unit square. So we need a permutation matrix of uncovered squares. The uncovered squares are n cells, one per row and column. The rest of the n^2-n cells must be covered by tiles, each tile is a rectangle not covering any uncovered square (i.e., cannot intersect the diagonal of the permutation). Tiles can be any rectangles that avoid the uncovered squares. The goal is to minimize number of tiles covering the rest.\n\nWe can think of covering the complement of a permutation matrix with rectangles that avoid the permutation points. This is reminiscent of covering a board with rook placements or something. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all cells except a permutation matrix is known: For a permutation matrix of size n, the complement can be partitioned into 2n-2 monotone rectangles? Actually there is known result: The complement of a permutation matrix can be partitioned into at most 2n-2 rectangles (monotone?). But the minimal number might be n? Let's think.\n\nWe can think of covering each row and column except the uncovered cell. The uncovered cells are at positions (i, p(i)). The complement consists of all cells (i,j) with j != p(i). For each row i, there are n-1 cells to cover. For each column j, there are n-1 cells to cover. The complement is a bipartite graph of size n x n minus a perfect matching. We want to partition the edges (cells) into rectangles (Cartesian product of row set and column set) that avoid the matching edges. This is like covering the complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching with complete bipartite subgraphs (i.e., bicliques). The minimum number of bicliques needed to cover all edges of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is known as the biclique cover number of the complement of a perfect matching. This is a known combinatorial problem: the bipartite graph complement of a perfect matching is the complete bipartite graph minus a perfect matching. The biclique cover number of K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is known to be n (maybe?). Let's recall known results: The biclique cover number of the complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} is 1 (the whole graph). But we cannot use the whole graph because it includes the matching edges. So we need to cover all edges except those n edges. The minimal number of bicliques needed to cover K_{n,n} \\ M (where M is a perfect matching) is known to be n (or maybe n-1?). Actually there is known result: The bipartite dimension (or bipartite covering number) of the complement of a perfect matching is n. Let's check: For each i, we can take the biclique formed by rows {i} and columns all except p(i). That's n bicliques (each row's all columns except its matched column). That uses n rectangles (each is a single row). But we can do better: we can use 2n-2 rectangles as in solution, but maybe we can use n rectangles: For each i, take row i except column p(i) (i.e., a row segment broken into two parts left and right of p(i)). But a row segment left of p(i) and right of p(i) are two separate intervals; we cannot have a single rectangle covering both left and right parts because they are not contiguous columns. But we could use a rectangle covering rows i..i (single row) and columns all except p(i) but columns must be contiguous interval. So we cannot cover the row with a single rectangle because the uncovered cell splits the row into two intervals. So each row requires at least two rectangles (unless the uncovered cell is at one end). But we can choose the permutation such that all uncovered cells are at the first column or last column? Actually we can choose the permutation to be identity (i,i). Then each row i has uncovered cell at column i, which splits the row into left part (columns 1..i-1) and right part (i+1..n). So each row would need two rectangles to cover left and right parts, unless we combine across rows. The solution uses vertical tiles (single column) to combine across rows: they use vertical tiles for each column, covering all rows above or below the diagonal. That yields 2(n-1) rectangles.\n\nBut perhaps we can reduce number of rectangles by using larger rectangles that span multiple rows and columns, not just single columns. For example, we could take a rectangle covering rows 1..k and columns k+1..n (above diagonal) which is a big rectangle covering many cells. Similarly, rectangle covering rows k+1..n and columns 1..k (below diagonal). This is a single rectangle for each k? Actually we could cover the entire upper triangle with one rectangle: rows 1..n-1, columns 2..n, but that rectangle includes diagonal cells (i,i) for i=2..n-1? Let's check: rectangle rows 1..n-1, columns 2..n includes cell (i,i) for i from 2..n-1 (since row i and column i are both in the ranges). So it includes diagonal cells, which is not allowed. So we need to avoid diagonal cells. So we cannot have a rectangle that includes any diagonal cell. So we need to partition the region above diagonal (ij) into rectangles that avoid diagonal. The region above diagonal is a strict upper triangular region, which is a set of cells where row index column index. This region is a \"staircase shape\". It can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles: For each k from 1 to n-1, we could take rectangle rows 1..k, columns k+1..n. That rectangle covers all cells where row <= k and column > k. Indeed, the union of these rectangles for k=1..n-1 covers the entire upper triangle exactly once (they are nested). However, these rectangles overlap: For k=1, rows 1..1, columns 2..n; for k=2, rows 1..2, columns 3..n; etc. The cell (1,3) is covered by both rectangle k=1 (since row 1, column 3) and rectangle k=2 (since row 1 <=2, column 3 >2). So they overlap, which is not allowed: each cell must be covered at most once. So we need a partition, not a covering with overlaps.\n\nWe could partition the upper triangle into \"staircase\" rectangles that are disjoint: For each i from 1 to n-1, we could take rectangle rows i..i, columns i+1..n (a single row covering all cells to the right of diagonal). That yields n-1 rectangles (each row's right part). Similarly, lower triangle can be partitioned into n-1 rectangles (each row's left part). That would be total 2n-2 rectangles, same as solution. But we could combine rows: For example, take rectangle rows i..j, columns j+1..n for some ij. This rectangle would cover cells where row between i and j, column > j. This region is a block of cells in the upper triangle that are to the right of column j. This block does not intersect diagonal because column > j >= row (since row <= j). So it's safe. This rectangle covers many cells across multiple rows. We can partition the upper triangle into such rectangles using a \"staircase decomposition\". For instance, we could take rectangle rows 1..n-1, columns n..n (single column n) covering cells (i,n) for i=1..n-1. That's one rectangle. Then rectangle rows 1..n-2, columns n-1..n-1 (single column n-1) covering cells (i,n-1) for i=1..n-2. Continue: each column j (except j=1) can be covered by a rectangle covering rows 1..j-1 (above diagonal). That's exactly the solution's vertical tiles: for each column k (2..n), tile B_k covering rows 1..k-1, column k. That's n-1 rectangles for upper triangle. Similarly for lower triangle: for each column k (1..n-1), tile A_{k+1} covering rows k+1..n, column k. That's n-1 rectangles for lower triangle. So total 2n-2.\n\nBut could we use fewer rectangles by using larger rectangles that combine multiple columns? For lower triangle, we could take rectangle rows k+1..n, columns 1..k (i.e., a block of size (n-k)*(k) covering all cells in lower triangle for columns up to k and rows > k. But this rectangle includes diagonal cells? Let's check: For any cell (i,j) in this rectangle, we have i > k >= j, so i > j, so it's below diagonal, safe. However, this rectangle includes cells from multiple columns (1..k). If we take such rectangle for each k, they will overlap. But maybe we can partition lower triangle into a few rectangles: For example, take rectangle rows 2..n, columns 1..1 (column 1 rows 2..n) - that's tile A_2. Then rectangle rows 3..n, columns 1..2 (columns 1 and 2) - but this includes column 1 rows 3..n which were already covered by tile A_2 (rows 2..n). Overlap again. So we need disjoint rectangles.\n\nOne approach: Partition lower triangle into \"staircase\" rectangles that are \"maximal\" in shape: For each row i (i>1), we could cover the left part of row i (columns 1..i-1) with a rectangle that includes rows i..i (single row). That yields n-1 rectangles for lower triangle. That's same as solution but using rows instead of columns. So 2n-2.\n\nBut perhaps we can use fewer rectangles by using both rows and columns in a clever way. Let's think about the bipartite graph covering problem: The complement of a perfect matching can be covered by n bicliques (complete bipartite subgraphs). Indeed, consider the following: For each i from 1 to n, define biclique B_i = (Rows {i, i+1, ..., n}) × (Columns {1, 2, ..., i-1}). This is exactly the tile A_{i} (for i from 2..n). That's n-1 bicliques. Similarly, we need bicliques for the other side: For each i from 1 to n, define biclique C_i = (Rows {1, 2, ..., i-1}) × (Columns {i, i+1, ..., n}). That's n-1 bicliques. So total 2n-2. But maybe we can combine some of these bicliques to reduce the total count. For instance, we could combine B_i and C_i into a single biclique? But they are disjoint sets of rows and columns, so cannot combine because they would intersect diagonal if we try to combine rows from both sides.\n\nBut maybe we can use a biclique that includes rows from both sides but columns also from both sides such that diagonal is avoided. For a biclique (R,C) to avoid diagonal, we need either all rows > all columns (R > C) or all rows all columns (R C). So any biclique must be either entirely above or entirely below diagonal. So we cannot have a biclique that includes rows both above and below diagonal relative to columns. So each biclique is either a \"lower\" biclique (rows > columns) or \"upper\" biclique (rows columns). The lower bicliques can be thought of as sets where min(R) > max(C). Similarly for upper.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the lower triangle region (i>j) with bicliques of type lower, and covering the upper triangle region (ij) with bicliques of type upper. The lower region is a Ferrers diagram shape (staircase). The minimal number of lower bicliques needed to partition the lower triangle is known to be the size of the antichain of the poset? Actually the lower triangle region is a Young diagram shape (a staircase). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram into rectangles (with sides aligned) is equal to the number of rows (or columns) maybe? Let's think.\n\nThe lower triangle region can be represented as a set of cells (i,j) with i>j. This shape is a staircase shape of size n-1 rows, where row i (i from 2..n) has i-1 cells (columns 1..i-1). The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition this shape into rectangles (with axis-aligned sides) is known to be n-1. Indeed, each row i has length i-1, and the shape is \"convex\" in the sense of Ferrers diagram. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Ferrers diagram into rectangles is equal to the number of distinct row lengths, which for this shape is n-1 (since row lengths are 1,2,...,n-1). However, there might be a more efficient partition using rectangles that span multiple rows and columns. For example, we can partition the lower triangle into n-1 rectangles by grouping rows with same column count? But the row lengths are distinct, so each rectangle can at most cover a region where the row lengths are constant. Since they are all distinct, we cannot combine rows of different lengths into a single rectangle without leaving uncovered cells or overlapping. Actually we can combine rows i and i+1 into a rectangle covering columns 1..i (since row i+1 has i cells, row i has i-1 cells). The rectangle covering rows i..i+1 and columns 1..i-1 would be a rectangle of size 2*(i-1). But row i+1 has an extra column i that would not be covered; we could cover that column i in a separate rectangle. So we could reduce the number of rectangles by using a rectangle that covers rows i..i+1 and columns 1..i-1, and then a rectangle covering rows i+1..i+1 and column i. This still uses 2 rectangles for rows i and i+1, not reducing count. In general, the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Ferrers diagram equals the number of \"corners\" or the number of distinct row lengths. For a staircase shape, that number is n-1. So lower triangle needs at least n-1 rectangles. Similarly upper triangle needs at least n-1 rectangles. So total at least 2n-2 rectangles. So the lower bound might be correct after all, but the argument given in the solution is flawed. However the lower bound might still hold due to a known combinatorial result: The bipartite dimension (biclique cover number) of the complement of a perfect matching is exactly 2n-2. Let's verify known results.\n\nThe biclique cover number (also known as bipartite dimension) of the graph K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching is indeed 2n-2. I recall a known result: The bipartite dimension of the complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} is ceil(log2 n). But that's for covering all edges with bicliques, not for covering all edges except a matching. Actually the bipartite dimension of a graph is the minimum number of complete bipartite subgraphs needed to cover all edges of the graph. For K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching, the bipartite dimension is n (maybe?). Let's check known results: The bipartite dimension of a complete bipartite graph K_{n,n} is ceil(log2 n). That's for covering all edges (including matching). But for K_{n,n} minus a perfect matching, perhaps the bipartite dimension is n? Let's think.\n\nBut the problem is not about covering edges with bicliques (which may overlap) but covering cells with rectangles that cannot overlap (each cell covered at most once). So it's a partition into rectangles, not a cover. So the minimal number of rectangles needed to partition the complement of a permutation matrix is known as the \"rectangle partition number\" of a permutation matrix complement. There is known result: The rectangle partition number of the complement of a permutation matrix is exactly 2n-2. This is known as the \"grid graph\" or \"permutation matrix complement\" problem. I recall a known combinatorial geometry problem: Partition the n x n board with one cell missing in each row and column into rectangles that avoid the missing cells. The minimal number of rectangles is 2n-2. This is a known problem: \"Covering a chessboard with rectangles avoiding a set of n rooks\" maybe. Actually there is known result: The minimum number of rectangles needed to partition an n x n board with a rook in each row and column (i.e., a permutation of squares) such that no rectangle contains a rook is 2n-2. This is a known theorem. So the solution's answer is correct, but the lower bound argument is flawed. However the lower bound can be proven by a different argument: each rectangle can contain at most one cell from each row and column? Actually a rectangle can contain multiple cells from a row and column, but cannot contain any diagonal cell. The lower bound of n-1 for each triangle is indeed correct: each column of the lower triangle must be covered by a distinct tile. But we found a counterexample: a tile below diagonal can cover multiple columns of L. Let's double-check: The tile is a rectangle defined by rows R = {r1,...,r2} and columns C = {c1,...,c2}. For it to be below diagonal, we need min(R) > max(C). So all rows > all columns. So if C includes multiple columns, say c1 c2, then rows must be > c2. So the rectangle includes all columns from c1 to c2. So it covers cells in columns c1,...,c2. So indeed a tile can cover multiple columns of L. So the claim that each column of L must be covered by a distinct tile is false. However, perhaps they meant each column of L must be covered by a distinct tile because each tile can only use one column of L due to the condition that rows must be > all columns, but they also require that rows are a contiguous interval and columns are a contiguous interval. If a tile uses columns c1..c2, then rows are > c2. So the tile covers column c1..c2. So it uses multiple columns. So the claim is false. But maybe they also require that each tile uses exactly one column of L because they also consider that each tile is either a vertical tile (single column) or a horizontal tile (single row). But they didn't restrict that; they just said \"tile cannot contain a diagonal cell, so rows and columns are disjoint\". They didn't restrict tile shape further. So the claim is false.\n\nThus the lower bound argument is invalid. However, the lower bound might still be true for other reasons. But the solution's justification for lower bound is flawed, which is a \"Critical Error\"? The lower bound is a key part of reasoning. If the argument is flawed, does that constitute a \"critical error\"? The solution claims that each column of L must be covered by a distinct tile, which is false. So the reasoning is invalid. However, the conclusion (lower bound) might still be true, but the reasoning is invalid. According to Level 2, we must check if reasoning uses valid mathematical principles. The principle used is \"each column of L must be covered by a distinct tile\" which is a claim that is false. This is a false claim, not just a missing justification. It's a logical error. That is a \"CRITICAL_ERROR\" at Level 2 because the reasoning includes a false statement that leads to a lower bound. The solution's lower bound argument is invalid, thus the reasoning is invalid. According to the hierarchical decision tree, Level 2 is about checking validity of methods, not correctness of claims. But a false claim is a critical error in reasoning. The method is \"argument by counting columns\" but the claim is false; the method is invalid because it uses a false premise. So we should classify as \"CRITICAL_ERROR\". However, the solution also provides a construction achieving the bound. The construction is valid. But the lower bound argument is flawed, so the proof of minimality is invalid. Thus the solution does not correctly prove minimality. The answer may still be correct, but the reasoning is not valid. According to the decision tree, if Level 2 fails (invalid reasoning), the verdict is FAIL.\n\nBut we need to examine if the lower bound argument can be salvaged: maybe they meant each column of L must be covered by a distinct tile because each tile can only use one column of L due to the condition that rows must be > all columns, but they also require that rows and columns are disjoint sets, but that doesn't restrict columns to be single. However, they also said \"any tile that covers a cell of column k in L must have its set of rows R contained in {k+1,...,n}; otherwise a row ≤ k together with column k would give a diagonal cell.\" That's correct. Then they say \"consequently a tile that uses column k can use only one column of L, because if it used two distinct columns kℓ then its rows would have to be >ℓ, contradicting the presence of column k.\" Let's examine that: If tile uses columns k and ℓ (kℓ), then rows must be >ℓ (since min(R) > max(C) = ℓ). That is consistent with covering column k because rows > ℓ > k, so rows > k as well. So no contradiction. So their statement \"contradicting the presence of column k\" is false. So the reasoning is indeed flawed.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound proof is invalid. So the solution fails at Level 2 (invalid reasoning). According to the hierarchical decision tree, we must output FAIL.\n\nNow we need to produce JSON output with fields: answer_correctness, confidence, issues (list), reasoning, verdict.\n\nWe must also include final answer and solution fields? Wait the final output format: The JSON schema includes fields: answer_correctness, confidence, issues, reasoning, verdict. Not solution and final_answer. The earlier instruction about \"Return your response as valid JSON with this exact structure: { \"solution\": \"...\", \"final_answer\": \"...\" }\" seems contradictory. Actually the initial instruction is to output the verification summary as JSON matching the provided schema. The schema is \"verification_verdict\". It includes fields: answer_correctness, confidence, issues, reasoning, verdict. So we need to output those fields.\n\nThus we need to produce JSON with those fields.\n\nNow we need to fill answer_correctness: Since it's an optimization problem, answer correctness is not determined at Level 1. According to the schema, answer_correctness can be \"CORRECT\", \"INCORRECT\", \"INCOMPLETE\", \"UNKNOWN\". Since we cannot determine if the answer is correct (minimum number of tiles) due to lack of ground truth, we should set answer_correctness to \"UNKNOWN\". The solution claims 4048. We cannot confirm it's correct. So answer_correctness = \"UNKNOWN\". Or maybe we can set \"UNKNOWN\" because we cannot verify optimality. The instructions for answer correctness: For optimization problems, Level 1 does not check correctness, but we still need to fill the field. The schema expects a value. Since we cannot determine, we set \"UNKNOWN\".\n\nConfidence: We need to assign a confidence level. Since we found a critical error in reasoning, we are confident that the solution is invalid. So confidence high, maybe 0.95.\n\nIssues: We need to list issues found. The main issue is the lower bound argument is invalid due to false claim that each column of L must be covered by a distinct tile. This is a critical error. We need to provide location (quote from solution) and description. Also maybe mention that the argument about tile using only one column is false. Also maybe mention that the lower bound argument is flawed, leading to invalid proof of minimality. That is a critical error (severity 8-10). Provide description.\n\nWe may also note any other issues? The construction appears valid, but the lower bound argument is flawed. So we have one critical error.\n\nNow reasoning: Summarize that the solution's construction is valid but the lower bound proof is incorrect because a tile can cover multiple columns of the lower triangle, so the claim that each column must be covered by a distinct tile is false. Therefore the reasoning is invalid, leading to a FAIL verdict.\n\nVerdict: \"FAIL\".\n\nNow produce JSON accordingly.\n\nWe need to ensure the JSON is valid, no extra fields. The fields: answer_correctness, confidence, issues, reasoning, verdict.\n\nNow we need to fill issues array: each issue object must contain type, location, description, severity. The type: \"CRITICAL_ERROR\". Location: a snippet from solution. Let's quote the part: \"Thus each column $k$ of $L$ must be covered by a distinct tile. Hence at least $n-1$ tiles are needed to cover the whole lower‑triangular part $L$.\" That is the false claim. Provide description: The claim that each column of the lower triangular region must be covered by a distinct tile is false because a single tile below the diagonal can cover multiple columns simultaneously; the argument that a tile using column $k$ can use only one column of $L$ is incorrect. Therefore the lower bound $2n-2$ is not justified.\n\nSeverity: 9 (critical error). Provide severity 9.\n\nNow confidence: 0.96.\n\nNow answer_correctness: \"UNKNOWN\". Or maybe \"UNKNOWN\" is appropriate.\n\nNow final JSON.\n\nBut we need to ensure we follow the schema: The schema expects \"answer_correctness\" as a string with values \"CORRECT\",\"INCORRECT\",\"INCOMPLETE\",\"UNKNOWN\". So we set \"UNKNOWN\". \"confidence\" as number between 0 and 1. \"issues\" as array of objects with fields type, location, description, severity. \"reasoning\" as string. \"verdict\" as \"FAIL\". That should be it.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\n"
