tly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  Consequently the set \\\\(U\\\\) of uncovered squares consists of \\\\(n\\\\) squares, one in each row and each column; thus \\\\(U\\\\) is a permutation matrix.  Write the column of the uncovered square in row \\\\(i\\\\) as \\\\(h_i\\\\) (so \\\\(h_i\\\\in\\\\{1,\\\\dots ,n\\\\}\\\\) and the values \\\\(h_1,\\\\dots ,h_n\\\\) are all distinct).\\n\\n---\\n#### 1.  Structure of a tile\\n\\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines, i.e. a set of cells\\n\\\\[R=\\\\{(r,c)\\\\mid r_1\\\\le r\\\\le r_2,\\\\;c_1\\\\le c\\\\le c_2\\\\}\\\\]\\nfor some integers \\\\(1\\\\le r_1\\\\le r_2\\\\le n\\\\) and \\\\(1\\\\le c_1\\\\le c_2\\\\le n\\\\).  Because no tile may contain a cell of \\\\(U\\\\), for every row \\\\(i\\\\) that belongs to the rectangle we must have either\\n\\\\[c_2 < h_i\\\\quad\\\\text{(the rectangle lies completely to the left of the hole in row }i\\\\text{)}\\\\]\\nor\\n\\\\[c_1 > h_i\\\\quad\\\\text{(the rectangle lies completely to the right of the hole in row }i\\\\text{)}.\\\\]\\nThus a rectangle that covers cells from two different rows must lie **entirely** on the same side (all left or all right) of the holes of those rows.\\n\\n---\\n#### 2.  Left‑hand intervals cannot be merged\\n\\nFor each row \\\\(i\\\\) define its *left interval* \\\\(L_i=[1,\\\\,h_i-1]\\\\) (empty when \\\\(h_i=1\\\\)) and its *right interval* \\\\(R_i=[h_i+1,\\\\,n]\\\\) (empty when \\\\(h_i=n\\\\)).  Any tile that covers cells of row \\\\(i\\\\) and lies to the left of the hole must contain the whole interval \\\\(L_i\\\\); otherwise some cell of \\\\(L_i\\\\) would remain uncovered.\\n\\nSuppose a single tile covers the left intervals of two distinct rows \\\\(i\\\\) and \\\\(j\\\\).  Its column range must be of the form \\\\([1,\\\\,b]\\\\) with \\\\(b\\\\ge h_i-1\\\\) and \\\\(b\\\\ge h_j-1\\\\).  Without loss of generality assume \\\\(h_i<h_j\\\\).  Then \\\\(b\\\\ge h_j-1\\\\ge h_i\\\\), so colum